course: dictionary
lesson: 4117
title: Glamorous and looking from outside
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Glamorous means very attractive and exciting, often in a rich, stylish, showy way. «The actress wore a glamorous silver dress to the premiere.» The dress is shiny, fancy and eye-catching, so glamorous describes how it looks.
explain: Glamorous can also describe a job or a life that seems exciting from the outside. «Her job on the cruise ships looks glamorous in photos.» The photos show sunny decks and parties, but the long shifts and hard work are not in the picture. So it may not be so glamorous in real life.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Ask: does this describe something fancy, exciting or special? Sometimes the sentence says the opposite. «Cleaning blocked drains is not very glamorous» means the job is ordinary or messy.
fact: Glamorous means very attractive and exciting, often in a rich, stylish or showy way.
fact: Glamorous is an adjective, so it can change to «more glamorous» and «most glamorous».
fact: The noun that goes with it is «glamour», the feeling of style and excitement around a person or thing.
fact: Spelling rule: when a word ends in -our, the u usually drops before -ous, so «glamour» becomes «glamorous», and «humour» becomes «humorous».
fact: Something glamorous can look better from outside than it really is.
exercise: «The actress wore a glamorous silver dress to the premiere.» What does glamorous mean? | *Very attractive and stylish | Quiet and plain | Old and dusty | Wet and muddy
exercise: Which spelling is correct for the adjective from «glamour»? | glamourous | *glamorous | glamorouse | glamorus
exercise: «Cleaning blocked drains is not very glamorous.» What does this suggest? | The drains are made of gold | *The job is ordinary or messy | The job is famous all over the world | The job is fancy and exciting
test: «Her cruise ship job looks glamorous in photos, but the shifts are long.» What does the sentence suggest? | The job is exactly as exciting as the photos show | *The job can look better from outside than it really is | The job is quiet and plain at all times | The job is only for children
test: Which word best fits the gap? «The party was ___, with silver lights and famous guests.» | *glamorous | tiny | hungry | muddy
exam: «The hotel lobby was glamorous, but the room upstairs was tiny and dull.» What does this tell you? | Glamorous means tiny | *Glamorous describes the fancy, showy look of the lobby, unlike the dull room | Glamorous describes the fancy, showy look of the lobby | Glamorous means dull and dusty
